  "passages": [
    {
      "id": "b2.lifelong-learning.back-to-school",
      "level": "B2",
      "topics": ["lifelong-learning"],
      "title": "Never too late",
      "text": "When Elena retired at sixty-eight, her grandchildren gave her a tablet. She could barely send a message, and she hated asking for help. \"I wish I had learned this years ago,\" she thought. Instead of giving up, she signed up for a free online course for beginners. Two years later, she has finished eleven courses, from photography to Italian. \"I'm not fluent yet,\" she laughs, \"but I can order dinner and argue about soccer.\" Elena is not unusual. More and more older adults are going back to learning, sometimes to keep up with technology and sometimes just for pleasure. Many of them are self-taught: they pick up new skills from videos, books and patient friends. Researchers say that learning in later life keeps the brain active and helps people feel connected to others. Elena's advice for anyone who thinks they are too old is simple: \"Don't wait until you have time. Start with something small that you enjoy, and do a little every day.\"",
      "source": {
        "kind": "original"
      }
    }
  ],
